.TH vapor 1 "January 2026" "VaporShell" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vapor \- ephemeral isolated shell session (optional encrypted workspace)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vapor
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIMODE\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
VaporShell launches an ephemeral shell environment isolated with Linux namespaces (Bubblewrap).
Sessions may be backed by a single-use encrypted workspace (Quixand) and may use Tor-oriented networking.

On exit, VaporShell attempts Quixand purge (key destruction / crypto-erase) and removes per-session directories.
Any plaintext overwrites are best-effort and not a provable secure wipe on modern storage.

VaporShell is intended as a security engineering proof-of-concept and a practical local sandbox wrapper.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
Launch an interactive mode selection menu.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-debug
Enable debug output to stderr.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage text and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Show version information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-tor-required
Fail closed: if Tor enforcement via network namespace is not possible, exit instead of falling back to proxy environment variables.
.TP
.BR \-\-leak-check
Run a best-effort IP/DNS/proxy sanity check inside the sandbox before starting the interactive shell.
.TP
.BR \-\-seccomp-bpf " " \fIFILE\fR
Pass a precompiled seccomp BPF filter to bubblewrap.
.SH MODES
.TP
.B default
Ephemeral isolated shell with Tor-oriented networking. Tor is enforced only when network namespace setup is available; otherwise behavior is best-effort.
.TP
.B paranoid
Maximum isolation: airgapped networking and hides \fI/proc\fR (proc replaced with tmpfs).
.SH SECURITY
.SS Isolation
VaporShell uses Bubblewrap namespaces and hardening flags including unshared user/pid/uts/ipc/cgroup namespaces, a new session, parent-death, a cleared environment, and a full capability drop.
.SS Data destruction
On exit, VaporShell attempts:
.IP \[bu] 2
Quixand purge (key destruction / crypto-erase) for the session workspace.
.IP \[bu] 2
Best-effort overwrites of plaintext artifacts under the session directory before removal.
.PP
Note: plaintext overwrites are not a provable secure wipe on SSD/NVMe, copy-on-write filesystems, journaling, snapshots, or backups. Crypto-erase via key destruction is the primary assurance.
.SS Tor routing
In \fBdefault\fR mode, VaporShell may enforce Tor routing using a network namespace when permitted. If namespace creation is not possible, it may fall back to proxy environment variables, which is best-effort.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /dev/shm/vaporshell-<id>/
Preferred per-session workspace directory (RAM-backed on most systems).
.TP
.I /tmp/vaporshell-<id>/
Fallback per-session workspace directory.
.TP
.I <session>/config/
Per-session Quixand configuration.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Launch default mode:
.B vapor
.TP
Launch paranoid mode:
.B vapor paranoid
.TP
Launch interactive menu:
.B vapor -i
.TP
Fail closed if Tor cannot be enforced:
.B vapor --tor-required
.TP
Run leak check and then enter the session:
.B vapor --leak-check
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Failure (dependency missing, preflight failure, workspace failure, or sandbox build failure).
.SH NOTES
If you see "no job control" warnings in containerized environments, ensure \fI/dev/pts\fR is available. This build binds \fI/dev/pts\fR into the sandbox to support interactive job control.
